A||N||32||tgowens||Rare 87 1/2 GT?||||1||
Z||000000||tgowens||03-01-2001||01:11 AM||||Well.........the GT itself isn't all that rare I suppose, it's a blue '87GT <I>but</I> it now has an '88 suspension on the rear.<P>Actually, George is fabricating a rear suspension similar to the '88 suspension for the '87 (or '86 or '85 or '84 (if upgraded to 14" wheels).........) -- this will not only virtually eliminate bump-steer <I>but</I> will allow for the use of the '88 rotors and calipers which now means, an upgraded brake system with the larger vented rotors while still using the stock size wheels!! Phase two of this upgrade will be for the front suspension.<P>So, this GT not only has the first production Fiero Shop hood vents but now the upgraded rear suspension and soon the Fiero Shop side vents...........my gosh, what will be next?? (I can think of a <I>few</I> things!!<P>------------------<BR>My Fiero Web Site.....<BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/index.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tgowens.gif">    </A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||03-01-2001||01:59 AM||||Sounds like you guys will be doing quite a bit of business with all these new products....<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wannabe||03-01-2001||09:01 AM||||What happened to the bump steer kit?||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||03-01-2001||01:01 PM||||the bump-steer kit will be incorporated into this suspension component <B>quote: -- this will not only virtually eliminate bump-steer but will allow for the use of the '88 rotors and calipers</B><p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 03-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bob Englert||03-01-2001||09:03 PM||||Just a question...<P>Why would I want to 'upgrade' my rear brakes to use parts that are very hard to find and significantly more expensive the the originals.  Wouldn't it make a lot more sense to create and upgrade that uses common, affordable, and available rear disk components that would allow us to keep the e-brake functions.<P>One reason I got rid of my Formula is that I didn't want to fight trying to trake down brake and suspension parts.  <P>Just wondering....<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||03-01-2001||09:11 PM||||Isn't that strange...I'm trying to get rid of my '87 GT and pick up an '88 GT or Formula so I won't have to go through the bizarre and extensive suspension mods required to make it competitive for road course time trials, and others are trying to dump them. This complaint has come up before. Is it really that hard to get parts for the '88s?||||reg||1
Z||000006||tgowens||03-01-2001||09:29 PM||||Wannabe, since the '88s were only made for one year with those suspension parts the answer is yes, they are harder to come by (probably the reason why I have three '87s!) <I>BUT</I> (and Bob, this is in answer to your question too) this suspension upgrade is <B>fabricated</B> not using stock '88 parts and as I said it will <B>allow</B> for the use of the '88 rotors and calipers, I did <B>NOT</B> say it was <I>required</I> it is planned to set this up to accept quite a few other rotors and calipers........one of the reasons for this unit is to provide better handling with virtually no bump-steer (as with the '88) and to allow the use a larger rotor and better caliper without having to change to a larger wheel just to accomodate the brakes!........and.......with this particular kit we are retaining the e-brake function..........||||reg||1
Z||000007||fieroshop||03-01-2001||09:32 PM||||Well since the cat is out of the bag, The kit I am building will have parts available at all times. A person will be able to get replacement parts from us. I will have all of the items in stock and on the shelves.<BR>I for one like the idea of this type of suspension and brake upgrade that this kit will accomplish. <BR>I am also doing this in a manner that hopefully will be affordable. <BR>Any and all comments will be greatly appreciated.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 03-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||DL10||03-01-2001||09:39 PM||||Does anyone know where to get a e-brake cable for a 88. I'm told their no longer around.||||reg||1
Z||000009||tgowens||03-01-2001||09:39 PM||||oh, and the idea here is to provide a "bolt-on" upgrade where the car owner does not have to do any modifying or fabricating of the stock cradle..........more than likely this unit (as I see it now) will retain the stock '87 (or '86 etc) knuckle and hub bolted to the strut -- the only part that will need to be removed will be the a-arm and the rear tie rod. This unit will bolt into the cradle in the same location as the a-arm. There will be a trailing arm which will also bolt on and off ya go -- no welding, no cutting, no drilling.........we are trying to make a unit which is easy to install, increase the performance and ride, and provide better options for brakes and wheels............<P>What we <I>really</I> want to know is if this interests you or are there too many drawbacks and negative aspects of this unit.........in other words.........if we build it, are you gonna buy it?? The way I see it is, we are trying to improve the performance and handling characteristics of the '87, '86, '85, and '84 Fiero by giving it the characteristics of an '88 which we all basically agree is superior to the other model years -- we are not using stock '88 parts which are hard to find - but, like I said - is this something that interests people? As for me, I plan to put it on my GTs........||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||03-01-2001||10:29 PM||||Sounds very cool!  I'd like to buy a kit, but it won't be for awhile.. too many other things I have to do to my car..||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||03-01-2001||11:12 PM||||Are you absolutly sure you want everyones input? <P>With the exception of certain racing applications vented rear rotors don't mean a flaming thing. Vented rear rotors can actually cause problems on vehicles not setup right. Rear brakes simply do not get the load that front brakes do. Unless you are really into driving backwards.  <P>I strongly recomend you talk to your lawyers and a very good liability insurance company before you release another hacked together brake system to the general public. Otherwise the first person hurt involving a car with your brakes on it is going to own your business and everything else they can get their hands on.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Black88GT||03-01-2001||11:20 PM||||Parts aren't hard to come by for 88s at all.  Its the price that gets people.  And personally, knowing that I have better stock parts then 93% of all Fiero's made is worth the extra money to me. <P>Back to the original post.  I think it was Ryane who made adapters for putting an 88 rear suspension on pre-88's, so its nothing new.  But this "fabricating a rear suspension similiar to the '88 suspension" is kinda fishy to me.  If you produced it and sold it you might get into trouble with Pontiac, depending on how "similiar" it is.  <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fieropage/af.bmp"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Will||03-01-2001||11:33 PM||||Sounds like a (cheaper?) version of the concept Ryane/Held used for their bump steer kit.<BR>Except for the trailing arm. Where/how is that going to attach without welding to the cradle? Forward cradle mount bolt?<BR>Do you really need a trailing arm with the control arm pivots spaced as far apart as they are longitudinally? Will it be a <I>functional</I> trailing arm with real anti-squat, or what?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Wannabe||03-01-2001||11:59 PM||||I think Ryane did a great job with their bumpsteer kit, with the exception of a few minor details. The challenge to a setup like this is sorting out the attachment points on the pre-88 hub/knuckle. Another challenge is sway bar end link mounting. By the time all that is sorted out, it may be just as cost effective, although not as simple, to do a complete cradle swap.<P>Would I buy it? If it is well though out, well built, and complete, there's a good chance I'd be in the market if I don;t find a good '88 first. Does the typical street driven, power boosted Fiero really need it? With what is already available out there, probably not. Unfortunately, there's not likely to be much demand for this type of kit unless it is very inexpensive. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||tgowens||03-02-2001||12:29 AM||||ok folks, I get the picture here -- thanks for your valued input -- theogre is right, vented rotors don't mean a flaming thing and that explains why so many cars on the road today have them. How silly of me not to realize that........and of course, that <I>is</I> what we do here at the Fiero Shop, produce "hacked together" brake systems.........of course I think he missed the point that <I>we</I> are not making the brakes.......<P>and as for the "similar" issue of the suspension.......sheesh......people everywhere try to improve on things.....here we are trying to improve on a car that was produced 15 years ago......and, it's not like we are the first to do so.......I'll consult with the lawyers of those who have produced parts and components for the Fiero over the past 15 years, surely they have precedence to cite.<P>So, you have all answered my question with your authority, wisdom, and opinionation.......for that I thank you.||||reg||1
Z||000016||87ECO5||03-02-2001||08:53 AM||||I applaud ANYONE that makes an effort to offer something that can/will significantly improve our cars - ESPECIALLY considering there's not a huge market for it!<P>Kudos, Terry and George!  I, for one, thank you!||||reg||14
Z||000017||theogre||03-02-2001||10:26 AM||||No. I didn't miss the point. You are making/marketing a brake package to use on a car that was not designed for it. If you really think you won't be held liable for problems with it, you're only kidding yourself. Now if the brake package was designed specifically for Fiero by someone at the level of Wilwood then that might offer some protection. This is the same problem people marketing the grand am upgrade kits have.<P>You are also marketing suspension parts. These you claim to be making yourself. You would face all the liability if a part you made breaks and someone wrecks. <P>I don't know where you get your information, I doubt I want to. Actually the majority of cars, including cars from some very expensive import lines, don't use vented rear rotors. Those that do have far less venting than front rotors. In Fiero's weight class vented rear rotors are the exception not the norm. I've spent the last year checking every rear rotor I could find. (Since I walk thru a public parking garage to get between buildings at work, that's allot of cars checked. Gotta love aluminum wheels.) Vented rears are few and far between, even on the recent models.<P>The fact you claim you'll always have parts for it doesn't mean much either. There are any number of reasons you could be out of busines tomorow. 99% of them are beyond your control. You could have an accident in the shop, an airplane could crash on you or anything in between. In 25 years in this business I'vee seen shops go out for all kinds of usually stupid reasons. How many cars got destroyed when some storage garage in another thread caved in. S___ happens.<P>But hey, I just make all this stuff up out of thin air. You just think whatever it is that you're thinking and when it bites you in the ___ then don't come whining back to everyone here.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||03-02-2001||11:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>ok folks, I get the picture here -- thanks for your valued input -- theogre is right, vented rotors don't mean a flaming thing and that explains why so many cars on the road today have them. How silly of me not to realize that........and of course, that <I>is</I> what we do here at the Fiero Shop, produce "hacked together" brake systems.........of course I think he missed the point that <I>we</I> are not making the brakes.......<P>and as for the "similar" issue of the suspension.......sheesh......people everywhere try to improve on things.....here we are trying to improve on a car that was produced 15 years ago......and, it's not like we are the first to do so.......I'll consult with the lawyers of those who have produced parts and components for the Fiero over the past 15 years, surely they have precedence to cite.<P>So, you have all answered my question with your authority, wisdom, and opinionation.......for that I thank you.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I am legitimately curious about your setup. More power to you for making it. Keep up the good work.<BR>I think theogre is a worry wart, don't let him get to you.<BR>Don't let the rest of these gainsayers get to you either. Even though they may profess to, the majority (or maybe just a vocal and obnoxious few) of the people on this board don't like <I>really</I> innovative thinking very much. I feel your pain.<BR>I didn't condemn your efforts. I'm sorry if you took it that way. I asked my questions because, well, I want to know.||||reg||1
Z||000019||tgowens||03-02-2001||12:27 PM||||Scott, I thank you!<P>Will, my statements weren't directed at you......<P>as for theogre......he does have <I>some</I> valid points........but, I would rather try to improve something ang fail than to be guilty of not trying at all........I will admit that I haven't done all of the brake calculations he has but then this isn't a scientific project, just a project to improve the handling and braking characteristics of a car that has been out of production for 14 years.<P>As for the liability, If I put it on my car I will assume the liability........I'm not one who will jump to litigation over a loose screw!<P>The way I see it is this, there are those who  <B>DO</B> things and there are those who <B>TALK</B> about them.........as for me, I'd much rather be a do'er......<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Black88GT||03-02-2001||12:27 PM||||Ever heard of Kick Hill Farms?  They had a barn that burned down, lost all kinds of stuff.  Luckily it wasn't there Fiero barn but still.  Like theogre said, sh!t happens.<P>I think that tgowens asked for everyone's opinion so thats what he got.  <P>P.S. If its priced along the same lines as the hood vent was, don't expect 100 orders a week.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||tgowens||03-02-2001||12:38 PM||||yes, I know of Bob at Kick Hill and I do know that things happen, that was unfortunate and a loss to Bob but, it wasn't the end of his world and he is still in business........the only guarantee you have in live is, if you were born you are going to die.......<P>As for the price of the hood vents........I can see that you are a talker rather than a do'er -- you have to take into account the cost of the materials as well as cost out the time to make the part as well as overhead - then add on a normal mark-up to make a profit to operate the business. Now, if I expected to give them away I might expect 100 orders a week but then I couldn't afford to produce the product very long, could I? -- It is a very simple matter, here is the product, here is what it will do, here is the price - you either buy it or you don't - there is no in between..........that's the way business is........it is very simple - you don't want to pay the price for a certain product you simply don't buy it! There are those who will and there are those who won't. I know how this works, I have been in the business world longer than most of you are old........<P>Yes, I asked for opinions, I did not, however, ask for snide remarks, rude comments, and severe sarcasm.........<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||Black88GT||03-02-2001||01:05 PM||||The point about Kick Hill was that you don't know what could happen to your business.<P>I didn't say that as a do'er or a talker, but as a consumer.  Everyone I have talked to said the same thing so I do not stand alone. They won't speak up though because they don't want to hurt anyones feelings.  ||||reg||1
Z||000023||tgowens||03-02-2001||01:25 PM||||I realize that no one knows what will happen to their business any more than they know what will happen to their car.......and even the Giant Pontiac abruptly stopped making a car in 1988...........but, your point is noted.<P>As for the hood vents........people pay $255 for the Meachem side scoops but won't pay $186.95 for hood vents........makes perfect sense to me......I wouldn't buy the Meachem scoops because I don't care for them not because of the price....if I wanted them badly enough I would pay the price they are priced at. If you and others don't want to buy the vents that is ok, that is called "personal choice". To say that the cost is too high..........maybe it is too high for YOU (and the others you refer to) but considering the time and materials, etc that went into that project it IS a fair price........we didn't just pour resin in a mold, we made the mold itself too - and we designed the project from the ground up.......that's ok with me though, the product works, there are only two of them like that in the world installed on Fieros at the moment and I like them.........that's what it's all about.<P>As for the suspension upgrade.....it is going on my car, if my car falls flat in the middle of the street, so be it - I will pick it up and fix it, since the upgrade is a bolt-on I can always go back to the old '87 suspension.....if it doesn't fall in the street, I may just have a better riding/handling car........the person who never takes a risk in life never does <I>anything</I>.......even getting up in the morning is a risk, isn't it?||||reg||1
Z||000024||stimpy||03-02-2001||02:23 PM||||Hey George and Terry, y'all have been on this board long enough to realize that any vendor who dares go outside the box and create something innovative subjects themselves to intense scrutiny and ridicule. Just ask V8Archie and Chris West. Perhaps it is best to just stick to marketing valve stem caps and reproduction 2M4 decals if you look to be universally cherished.<P>I say f*ck the naysayers and do what you need to do.    ||||reg||1
Z||000025||tgowens||03-02-2001||02:51 PM||||stimpy!! - you are possessed with ESP!! I am working on a valve stem upgrade even as we speak!! I am putting vents in the valve caps but I realize that it will facilitate a slow leak until it gets gunked up with road grime........on well, back to the drawing board........i'll make sure that you get the first set though!!<P>Nawwwww, i don't let them bother me - no matter what ya do there are always those out there who think they know better or can do better -- I never try to change their mind but rather get them to look at things a different way........reality is........that's what makes the world go around, a difference of opinion and subjective ideas -- it's just when one tries to influence others to their way of thinking that I get upset.....giving a personal opinion is just that - one person's opinion as they have spoken it......I don't get on here and criticize others because they think they might have something that will improve these cars.........that <I>should</I> be what this Forum is all about........improving the car and helping others.........but, there <I>are</I> those who will always throw out their two cents like I am some newbie who doesn't know better.......hell, I'm 54 years old and have taken many chances in life because I dared to be a little different and try things.......I'm not gonna sit around and tell others why they shouldn't!<P>Now, about that valve stem upgrade, you want the upgrade for all four wheels or do you want to try one at a time? and........I can make them in gold to match your car.......!<P><p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||stimpy||03-02-2001||03:07 PM||||Just make sure they have DOT approved threading. I dont want to be responsible when one of them goes flying off and puts an eye out!||||reg||1
Z||000027||tgowens||03-02-2001||03:21 PM||||what's threading?||||reg||1
Z||000028||stsmithgt||03-02-2001||03:51 PM||||Hey Terry! Do those valve cap upgrades have a scoop on them!  I might be interested. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000029||tgowens||03-02-2001||04:23 PM||||nope, no scoops.......they do, however, come with a free pine tree air freshener and a discount on a new steering wheel (I'll get YOURS yet!!!)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000030||Bob Englert||03-02-2001||09:29 PM||||Interesting thread....<P>I think many of Ogres comments are valid.  tgowens says that if he puts a suspension upgrade on his car he accepts responsibility.  I agree that is the right thing to do but the courts are full of folks that don't take responsibility for their actions and are looking for someone else to take the fall.  If a installed suspension part were to fail (due to a design, manufacturing, or user installation problem) and causes serious injury many folks first impulse will be to call their lawyer.  I don't say this is right but is is a sad fact of our society today.  <P>I do agree that you should talk to a lawyer about product and business liability before offering products that might affect the operation of significant safety systems such as suspension or brakes.  Please understand that I am not saying don't do it, just be careful so you will be around for a long time to support the Fiero community!<P>I also suspect that a lot of owners will say go for it but most will be reluctant to spend the serious money that a kit that you describe will probably cost.  I personally would love to have a Ryane style suspension upgrade but I can't really justify spending almost much on suspension and brakes as the car is worth.  <P>Since I don't race my GT's, I am looking for products and/or modifications that will improve the reliability and extend the life of my car.  Providing repalcement parts that aren't available now (like some of the products from Rodney Dickman) are the things that would be most helpful to me.<P>Good luck with the new products.  I look forward to hearing about them as they come together.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||Black88GT||03-02-2001||09:35 PM||||tgowens, I never said it wasn't a fair price.  The mecham scoops replaced the whole body panel, the only installation was changing the panel (and paint of course).  But yours is more involved.  If you want to sell it for 5,000 bucks, its your call, but don't cry when no one buys them.<P>And no one was throwing out there 2 cents.  You ASKED for it.  Not only once, but twice.<BR>Just because you didn't get the flowery happy-go-lucky responses you were hoping for doesn't mean that you should come out swingin' with your following replies.<P>I don't think I can do better, and I'm not a naysayer.  I just happen to agree with theogre.  I love how these labels are thrown around when a difference of opinion comes up. No one discourages Chris West or Archie or any other vendor when they propose things, they just ask questions and pose problems that might arouse.  Whats wrong with that?  Like if Archie were to say tomorrow I am coming out with a kit for the V-10 Viper engine to be put into Fiero's, wouldn't you expect some "naysayers" (as you call them) to ask questions like "Wouldn't that cause serious suspension problems?" <P>SO, my point is, in the near future don't ask for peoples opinions unless your prepared to hear some less then favorable responses.  <P><p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||theogre||03-02-2001||11:57 PM||||Since I got sick last year my patience is really thin. I wasn't meaning to sound snotty.<P>In all fairness.... TG is right about the prices of making custom parts. It's not cheap to make a part consistently. It actually cost more to do it at low volume. Development cost must also be figured into it. I know this because many of my customers are large manufacturing companies. at $180 and change no one is getting rich off the hood parts.<P>Terry, we're talking about 2 diferant kinds of liability. At the moment you are thinking one off mod to your own car. If it breaks the only person you can yell at is you. If it breaks on the road and causes a wreck involving other people it's still you and your auto insurance.<P>Before you actually sell this you need to think the same way as big companies do about product liability. IF a part like a scoop breaks no one but the owner really cares but if brakes or suspension quit all hell will break loose. It's often not the person you sold the part to that you have to worry about. If some product you sell breaks or causes harm, you can have all sorts of people after you. You could have any one involved with another car or property. you could have the buyers insurance company. You could also have various state and federal agencies involved. (Am I a worry wart on some things? You bet. I'd like very much not to have any dead forum members or people dead because of...)<P><BR>None of this is saying the brakes can't be altered. <I>It's all in how it is done.</I> IF you don't work with the whole system, you open a can of worms so big it's not funny. Terry says it's not scientific but that's exactly what it has to be with some art thrown on top. Tiny changes to the system can have huge results.  Allot of the time the math just gives a rough idea of the direction you're going in. Since we don't have the specifics for things like the brake pads, it's imposible to know the full result untill the change is tested. If the hydraulic or rotor mechanical math is showing bad results to start with, things aren't likely to get better as you go along. <P>Let's just use the Grand Am upgrade as an example. This is a parts bin job someone originally developed for a race application. I'm sure it works well for them but we all know there are a bunch of problems with it on the street. (Ignoring hand brake right now.) Several people in the forums have said they aren't really impressed with the results and a few are considering removing it altogether.<P>Fact is that Grand Am parts or something along those lines can be done on the front to work properly and gain significant brake power AND play nice with the OE MC and rear brakes. Part of this involves a minor plumbing change between the MC and Balance block. (Once the system is reprop'd you'll also get more power at the rear axle.)<P>The mechanical issues of mounting the GA parts can probably be worked out but won't be as cheap or simple as people think. Reproportioning the system will always be as much art as science.<P>Is it worth it to fix the remaining GA front axle problems? Depends on how valuable people think that extra pressure is worth. If you put 400 pounds into the OE MC the extra pressure is over 1000 pounds of clamping force. That's a pretty large gain. Once the Front is setup and the system reproportioned there will also be power gained in the OE rears.<P>There's also the pending development to use a smaller diameter MC on an otherwise stock car. This gives us a pressure increase at both axles for the cost of the MC and a couple short brake lines. That has now advanced to the point of finding a suitable MC, which may be an 88 Fiero one or one from an S10 Truck. Once the deatials are worked out that change will take about 1-2 hours and be completely reversable in the same amount of time. (Unless your bleeder screws are shot...)||||reg||1
